# Every text file is stored with LF and checked out with LF, on every
# platform, whatever the local core.autocrlf happens to be.
#
# Two things depend on it. Biome's formatter writes LF and has no setting
# that would make it accept anything else, so a CRLF checkout fails
# `npm run format:check` on files nobody has touched. And tools/test-shell.sh
# is run by a shell, which reads a CRLF line ending as part of the
# interpreter path and refuses to start.
#
# text=auto still leaves git to detect content it should not touch, so the
# two binary rules below are belt-and-braces rather than load-bearing.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.ico binary
